  "primary_unit": "ton",
  "why": "Shop fabrication of structural steel is a genuinely multi-trade activity - raw sections and plate are cut and prepared, members are fitted/assembled, welded, then blasted and shop-primed. Each operation has a distinct crew and productivity, so a single mh/ton figure hides where the hours actually go. Norms are medium-complexity (beams, columns, bracing, base/cap plates, cleats); very light lattice/truss work or heavy plate girders shift toward the high/low bands respectively.",
  "assumptions": [
    "Medium fabrication complexity (typical building/industrial frame)",
    "Normal shop access with overhead travelling crane",
    "Single 8 hr/day shift",
    "Welding is manual/semi-automatic (MMA/FCAW/MAG)",
    "Shop primer only (finish coat applied on site or excluded)"
  ],
  "exclusions": [
    "Raw material supply price (quantity only carried)",
    "Detailing/shop drawing engineering hours",
    "Galvanizing",
    "Site painting / finish coats",
    "Rework and NDT-driven repairs",
    "Complex node/box fabrication beyond medium complexity"
  ],
